tan θ. Then m=tan 2θ=2n/(1-n²), while m=4n. Nonhorizontality gives n≠0, so 4(1-n²)=2 and n²=1/2. Hence mn=4n²=2.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1980_AMC_12_Q12","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h165","cnm_h069"],"id":"tangent-addition","label":"Translate the angles into slopes","methodDirectNodeIds":{"tangent-addition":["cnm_h166","cnm_h072"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h166","cnm_h072","cnm_h165","cnm_h069"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"adjudication":"Root reviewed the displayed task, definedness and stated conventional interpretation.","derivedAnswer":"D, 3*pi","reason":"The two roots of t^2-9t+1 are distinct positive reciprocals. Their principal acute angles alpha,beta are complementary, so alpha+beta=pi/2. The four solutions in (0,2*pi) are alpha,beta,alpha+pi,beta+pi, and their sum is 3*pi.","recordedAnswer":"D","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":["all_tangent_solution_branches_on_stated_interval"],"questionId":"1989_AMC_12_Q28","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h069","cnm_276"],"id":"reciprocal-tangents","label":"Use reciprocal tangent values","methodDirectNodeIds":{"reciprocal-tangents":["math:inverse-tangent-and-solutions","cnm_223","cnm_226"]},"nodeIds":["math:inverse-tangent-and-solutions","cnm_223","cnm_226","cnm_h069","cnm_276"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A: 0","reason":"Set a_n=tan(πr_n/12), with r_1=3, r_2=2, and r_(n+2)≡r_n+r_(n+1) modulo 12. The initial pair returns after 24 terms, no residue is 6, and r_17=0, so the recurrence has no poles. Since 2009≡17 modulo 24, |a_2009|=0.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2009_AMC_12A_Q25","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"tangent-angle-residue-cycle","label":"Use tangent addition","methodDirectNodeIds":{"tangent-angle-residue-cycle":["cnm_h072","cnm_h064","cnm_h069","math:modular-congruence"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h072","cnm_h064","cnm_h069","math:modular-congruence"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":6,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A (-4/3)","reason":"Writing s=sin x and c=cos x gives sc=-12/25 and (s-c)²=49/25. On the stated interval s>0 and c<0, so s-c=7/5. Therefore s=4/5, c=-3/5, and tan x=-4/3.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1978_AMC_12_Q15","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h060","cnm_148","cnm_075","cnm_h069"],"id":"solution:recover-sine-and-cosine:4a07892fdce3","label":"Method 1: Recover sine and cosine","methodDirectNodeIds":{"recover-sine-and-cosine":["cnm_h062","cnm_h061","cnm_220","cnm_090"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h062","cnm_h061","cnm_220","cnm_090","cnm_h060","cnm_148","cnm_075","cnm_h069"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h060","cnm_148","cnm_h069","cnm_040"],"id":"solve-for-tangent","label":"Method 2: Solve directly for tangent","methodDirectNodeIds":{"solve-for-tangent":["cnm_h062","cnm_h061","cnm_224"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h062","cnm_h061","cnm_224","cnm_h060","cnm_148","cnm_h069","cnm_040"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":6,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E, 8","reason":"Put s=sin(x), c=cos(x), and r=c/s. Geometricity gives c^3=s^2, so c^2(1+c)=1. Also a4=1 and r^2=1/c, hence a8=r^4=1/c^2=1+c. These relations force 0<c<1 and |r|>1, so the index is unique.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2010_AMC_12B_Q20","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h197","cnm_h199","cnm_h060","cnm_h069"],"id":"fourth-term-and-ratio","label":"Start from the fourth term","methodDirectNodeIds":{"fourth-term-and-ratio":["cnm_h207","cnm_h062","cnm_h208"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h207","cnm_h062","cnm_h208","cnm_h197","cnm